    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "SMA has been the focus of numerous previous structural studies, including a comprehensive fracture characterization project; however, the complete mechanics of faulting, folding, fracturing, and related deformation remains poorly understood. We use a high resolution digital elevation model (DEM) produced by airborne laser swath mapping (ALSM) to measure stratigraphic thickness of 4 folded subunits within the Phosphoria limestone formation at Sheep Mountain anticline (SMA), Wyoming, with the intent of identifying if, and to what degree, strata are thinned in the fold limbs. Bedding plane outcrops of 6 resistant units were field mapped onto the ALSM data and (x,y,z) points representing these outcrops were extracted, providing dense and precise constraint on orientation and location of folded strata. These data are used for direct measurement of stratigraphic thicknesses at diverse points on the fold, and to constrain interpolated 3D surface models of fold geometry in an attempt to obtain continuous measurement of stratigraphic thickness changes. Thickness measurements are analyzed in respect to location on the fold (forelimb vs. backlimb and local fold amplitude) in an effort to identify thickness changes due to folding. By quantifying stratigraphic thickness changes due to the folding process, we hope to quantify potential mass transport and gain insight about associated inelastic (e.g. plastic or viscous) deformation. This understanding will help to construct and assess results of future finite element models of SMA and the underlying thrust faults.",
